<h2 class="bde-heading-506-128 bde-heading">
Why Is Mike Conley Paid $3.88m?
</h2><div class="bde-fancy-container-506-129 bde-fancy-container">
  
  
	



<div class="bde-fancy-container-506-145 bde-fancy-container">
  
  
	



<div class="bde-f-text-506-158 bde-f-text">
<p>Conley is on the minimum because the market prices what a player will give you next season, and at 39 nineteen previous ones count for very little.</p>
<ul>
<li>He can still run an offense and make the right pass, which is what a bench unit on a contending team needs.</li>
<li>Point guards lose value fastest with age, because speed and the ability to stay in front defensively go before shooting does.</li>
<li>He earned above $26m a season for five years in <a href="/teams/memphis-grizzlies/">Memphis</a> and <a href="/teams/utah-jazz/">Utah</a>, so the money has already been made and a minimum deal costs him little.</li>
<li>Veterans at this stage generally pick a club and let the number follow, and Boston can offer minutes on a roster that expects to compete.</li>
<li>Minimum contracts for long-service players are partly reimbursed by the league, which makes signing him cheaper for the club than the figure implies.</li>
</ul>
<p>Boston gets a 20-year veteran for less than it pays several of its rookies.</p>

</div><div class="bde-shortcode-506-159 bde-shortcode"><div class="nba-fin" data-nba-fin="why">
